Here's a JavaScript function that swaps the positions of adjacent words in a sentence, effectively reversing the order of adjectives:

```javascript
function reverseAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(' '); // Split the sentence into an array of words
  for (let i = 0; I < words.length - 1; i++) {
    const firstWord = words[i];
    const secondWord = words[i+1];
    words[i] = secondWord;
    words[i+1] = firstWord;
  }
  return words.join(' '); // Join the reversed words back into a sentence
}

const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const reversedSentence = reverseAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(reversedSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

In this implementation, we first split the input `sentence` into an array of individual words using the `split()` method. Then, we loop through this array from index 0 up until the second-last index (i.e., the last word is skipped), swapping each pair of adjacent words using destructuring assignment. Finally, we join the reversed array back into a string and return it. This function will correctly swap the positions of all adjacent words in the input sentence, regardless of whether they are adjectives or not.

