response:
To make this solution more efficient for handling large amounts of text, you can consider using memory-mapped files instead of reading the entire contents of `Text1` and `Text2` into memory at once. This approach allows you to access the data as if it were loaded into memory, but only a portion of it is actually loaded at any given time. Here's how to modify the previous implementation using memory-mapped files:

```matlab
%% Memory-mapped files approach
filename1 = 'Text1.txt';
filename2 = 'Text2.txt';

%Create memory-mapped files with mmap function
mm1 = mmap(filename1, 'r', '0644');
mm2 = mmap(filename2, 'r', '0644');

%Set up pointers to start positions of text1 and text2
ptr1 = mmseek(mm1, 0, 'bof');
ptr2 = mmseek(mm2, 0, 'bof');

%Loop through text1 and text2 until end is reached
while true
    %Read a chunk of size 'chunkSize' from each file
    chunk1 = mmread(mm1, 'uint8', [ptr1 chunkSize]);
    chunk2 = mmread(mm2, 'uint8', [ptr2 chunkSize]);

    %Convert chunks to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = strsplit(lower(char(chunk1)), ' ');
    words2 = strsplit(lower(char(chunk2)), ' ');

    %Find unique words in this chunk
    uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

    %Update pointer positions based on chunk sizes
    ptr1 = ptr1 + chunkSize;
    ptr2 = ptr2 + chunkSize;

    %Check if we have reached the end of either file
    if mmsize(mm1) - ptr1 <= 0 || mmsize(mm2) - ptr2 <= 0
        %Close memory-mapped files and free resources
        mmclose(mm1);
        mmclose(mm2);
        mmunmap(mm1);
        mmunmap(mm2);

        %Print unique words found so far
        disp(uniqueWords);

