To resolve this issue, you can add appropriate error handling and input validation to your code. Here's an example of how you could modify the `RetrieveInputExample` class to handle this exception:

```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int num1 = 0;
        int num2 = 0;
        try {
            System.out.print("Enter first number: ");
            num1 = scanner.nextInt();
            System.out.print("Enter second number: ");
            num2 = scanner.nextInt();
            int sum = num1 + num2;
            System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter integers only.");
            scanner.nextLine(); // Consume any remaining input on the line
            main(args); // Recursively call main() to prompt user for input again
        }
        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

In this modified version, we wrap the input reading and calculation inside a try-catch block to catch the `InputMismatchException`. If such an exception is caught, we print an error message and consume any remaining input on the current line using `scanner.nextLine()`. We then recursively call `main()` to prompt the user for input again. This approach ensures that the program doesn't terminate abruptly due to the exception and instead provides meaningful feedback to the user. You may need to adjust the error message and input validation logic based on your specific use case.

