To implement bubble sort for sorting the given array in ascending order using C++, you can follow these steps:

1. Define a function `bubbleSort` to perform bubble sort.

2. Inside the `bubbleSort` function, take the array and its size as arguments.

3. Set a flag `swapped` to keep track of whether any swaps were made during a single iteration of the loop. If no swaps are made, then the array is already sorted.

4. Iterate over the length of the array using a `for` loop.

5. Within the inner loop, iterate from the beginning of the array up to the last unsorted element (i.e., the end of the current iteration).

6. Compare adjacent elements and swap them if they are in the wrong order.

7. After each inner loop iteration, check if any swaps were made. If not, break out of the outer loop since the array is now sorted.

Here's the code for the `bubbleSort` function:

```c++
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped = true;
    for (int I = 0; I < n - 1 && swapped; i++) {
        swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < n - I - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

